    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `GenerateSchema._sequence_schema`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `_sequence_schema` function generates a schema for a sequence type, such as `Sequence[int]`, which is used to validate and serialize sequence data in Pydantic models. It takes a single parameter, `items_type`, which specifies the type of elements in the sequence. The function first generates a schema for the item type using the `generate_schema` method. It then creates a list schema for the sequence items and copies it to use as the JSON schema. A Python schema is created to check if the input is an instance of `typing.Sequence`. If the item type is not `Any`, it adds a sequence validator to the Python schema using the `sequence_validator` function. Serialization is handled by wrapping a serializer function that converts the sequence to a list. Finally, the function returns a combined JSON or Python schema that includes both the JSON and Python schemas and the serialization logic. The function relies on several helper functions and methods, including `generate_schema`, `list_schema`, `smart_deepcopy`, `is_instance_schema`, `chain_schema`, `no_info_wrap_validator_function`, `wrap_serializer_function_ser_schema`, and `json_or_python_schema`. The function does not handle errors or edge cases explicitly, as it assumes valid input and relies on the underlying schema generation and validation mechanisms to handle any issues. There are no side effects in this function.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names